//? Title: Compare pooled and group-specific probabilities with WAIC
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en

> Score the same observations under two posterior models

Two groups have six binary outcomes each. The pooled model assigns one
success probability to both groups. The group-specific model assigns one
probability to each group. With uniform beta priors, the posterior shapes
are one plus the success and failure counts for each probability.

Each matrix row holds the log likelihood of every outcome at one posterior
draw. WAIC computes the log of the mean likelihood separately for each
outcome and subtracts the sample variance of its log likelihoods. A lower
deviance-scale WAIC indicates higher estimated predictive accuracy.

```{cpp, id=pooling-waic, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <datamunge/bayes/diagnostics.hpp>
#include <datamunge/random/simplex.hpp>
#include <algorithm>
#include <array>
#include <cmath>
#include <print>

int main() {
    using namespace datamunge;
    constexpr std::array outcomes{1, 1, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 0, 0};
    constexpr std::size_t draws = 12000;
    random::SplitMix64 rng(42);
    const auto log_likelihood = [&](double first, double second) {
        std::vector<double> row;
        for (std::size_t i = 0; i < outcomes.size(); ++i) {
            const double probability = i < 6 ? first : second;
            row.push_back(outcomes[i] ? std::log(probability) : std::log1p(-probability));
        }
        return row;
    };

    std::vector<std::vector<double>> pooled, by_group;
    for (std::size_t s = 0; s < draws; ++s) {
        const double common = random::dirichlet_sample(rng, {7, 7}).front();
        const double first = random::dirichlet_sample(rng, {6, 2}).front();
        const double second = random::dirichlet_sample(rng, {2, 6}).front();
        pooled.push_back(log_likelihood(common, common));
        by_group.push_back(log_likelihood(first, second));
    }

    const auto print_score = [](const char* model, const bayes::WaicResult& score) {
        const auto flagged = std::ranges::count_if(score.pointwise, [](const auto& point) {
            return point.effective_parameters > 0.4;
        });
        std::println("| {} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {:.2f} | {} |", model,
                     score.log_predictive_density, score.effective_parameters, score.waic, flagged);
    };
    std::println("| Model | Log predictive density | Effective parameters | WAIC | Variance above 0.4 |");
    std::println("| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |");
    print_score("Pooled", bayes::waic(pooled));
    print_score("By group", bayes::waic(by_group));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| Model | Log predictive density | Effective parameters | WAIC | Variance above 0.4 |
| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Pooled | -8.32 | 0.96 | 18.56 | 0 |
| By group | -5.64 | 1.49 | 14.27 | 2 |
// result_end

The group-specific model has the lower estimated WAIC. The uncommon
outcome in each group exceeds the 0.4 variance threshold, so its estimated
predictive advantage warrants closer examination.

[Vehtari, Gelman and Gabry (2017), section 2.2|https://doi.org/10.1007/s11222-016-9696-4]
